{"parentUuid":"7876bffc-0100-4f2b-bb9b-93faba845a88","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a15189ea6dd761cfe","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01HScmxhuGBVPrLXaDoHhDD7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Gb976ZCtaf36bx2pFhAHnf","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Bitwuzla SMT solver bit-vector 2025 benchmark performance"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1400,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5220,"cache_read_input_tokens":21205,"output_tokens":290,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5220},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1400,"output_tokens":290,"cache_read_input_tokens":21205,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5220,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5220,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaorigVLULSL6AipNvPH5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"120a0c80-dd46-4bf6-a61f-d6dc0587a2c1","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:28.519Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"f2cbeb28-9c79-4d02-873f-6055ae036006","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a15189ea6dd761cfe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DmQ17KH4Fb2JvzngysKMA9","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Z3 push pop incremental solving binding subproblem performance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"push and pop · Z3Prover/z3 · Discussion #5046\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/discussions/5046\"},{\"title\":\"Performance Issues with Incremental Solving · Issue #7441 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/7441\"},{\"title\":\"Push-Pop resulting in suspicious delay in solving · Issue #1141 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/1141\"},{\"title\":\"Programming Z3\",\"url\":\"https://z3prover.github.io/papers/programmingz3.html\"},{\"title\":\"On Incremental Pre-processing for SMT | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-38499-8_3\"},{\"title\":\"Python API: The overhead of pop() has been increasing · Issue #4995 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/4995\"},{\"title\":\"z3 incremental solving clarification · Issue #2220 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/2220\"},{\"title\":\"Control whether to use incremental or \\\"bulk\\\" SAT solver · Issue #248 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/248\"},{\"title\":\"The combined solver, incremental solving, and activation literals · Issue #501 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/501\"},{\"title\":\"Making Incremental SMT Solving Work for Logic ...\",\"url\":\"https://cs.pomona.edu/~michael/papers/iclp2020_sub.pdf\"}]\n\nThe search results provide relevant information about Z3's push/pop incremental solving and performance considerations. Here's a summary of the key findings:\n\n## Z3 Push/Pop Incremental Solving Overview\n\nZ3 solvers maintain a set of formulas and supports scope management where formulas added under one scope can be retracted when the scope is popped.\n\n## Performance Characteristics\n\n**Push/Pop Trade-offs:**\nPush/pop can amortize some overhead, but then also prevent some global simplifications. The performance impact depends on your use case. In non-incremental mode, Z3 can apply powerful rewrites to the whole SMT instance that may be destructive while maintaining equisatisfiability, but in incremental solving, these destructive rewrites may not be applicable because Z3 cannot be certain if assertions will be removed later.\n\n**Theory-Dependent Performance:**\nFor pure bit-vector constraints, the incremental and non-incremental solvers use different pre-processing and backend engines, with the non-incremental bit-vector solver usually being faster, whereas for linear integer arithmetic, the incremental solver seems to work very well.\n\n## Known Performance Issues\n\nSeveral users have reported performance problems with push/pop operations:\n- The pop() function in the Python API can become slower over repeated iterations, taking only milliseconds initially but increasing to hundreds of seconds.\n- Push/pop operations can cause dramatic slowdowns, with queries taking over 1000 seconds compared to just 0.23 seconds without push.\n\n## Incremental Pre-processing\n\nRecent research has addressed these limitations. A calculus for incremental pre-processing identifies when powerful formula simplifications can be retained when adding new constraints, enabling use cases that could not be solved in incremental mode.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"b336ef1d-88f3-4588-baf0-43677096ce1c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:34.715Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f2cbeb28-9c79-4d02-873f-6055ae036006","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"604dac6c-43d4-4de7-9247-0df0046dfade","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a15189ea6dd761cfe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TausQpGu21M3AwQGfBDxN8","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"cvc5 Python binding pythonic API tutorial example 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Basic cvc5 tutorial (pythonic API) - HackMD\",\"url\":\"https://hackmd.io/@s-fish/H1nqUvx6j\"},{\"title\":\"Quickstart Guide — cvc5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cvc5.github.io/docs/cvc5-1.2.1/api/python/pythonic/quickstart.html\"},{\"title\":\"API Documentation — cvc5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cvc5.github.io/docs/cvc5-1.0.0/api/api.html\"},{\"title\":\"Python API — cvc5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cvc5.github.io/docs/cvc5-1.1.2/api/python/python.html\"},{\"title\":\"Datatypes — cvc5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cvc5.github.io/docs/latest/api/python/pythonic/dt.html\"},{\"title\":\"cvc5 · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/cvc5/\"},{\"title\":\"Hello World — cvc5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cvc5.github.io/docs/cvc5-1.0.0/examples/helloworld.html\"},{\"title\":\"Python API · Issue #1522 · cvc5/cvc5\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/CVC4/CVC4/issues/1522\"},{\"title\":\"Theory Combination — cvc5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cvc5.github.io/docs/cvc5-1.0.0/examples/combination.html\"},{\"title\":\"Pythonic API — cvc5 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cvc5.github.io/docs/cvc5-1.0.2/api/python/pythonic/pythonic.html\"}]\n\nI found comprehensive information about cvc5's Python binding and pythonic API. Here's what the search results show:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe cvc5 pythonic API lets you input your constraints into the solver using Python. This is nice because by default, cvc5 input files must be written in specialized languages such as SMT-LIBv2.\n\n## Two Python API Options\n\ncvc5 offers two separate APIs for Python users. The base Python API is an almost exact copy of the C++ API. Alternatively, the pythonic API is a more pythonic API that aims to be fully compatible with Z3s Python API while adding functionality that Z3 does not support.\n\nIf you are a new user, or already have an application that uses Z3's python API, use the pythonic API.\n\n## Quick Start Example\n\nThe pythonic API provides a simpler interface. You can introduce variables using: x, y = Reals('x y') and a, b = Ints('a b')\n\nThen you can solve constraints like: solve(0 < x, 0 < y, x + y < 1, x <= y)\n\n## Available Documentation\n\nAdvanced programmers may prefer to consult the cvc5 documentation directly. The official cvc5 documentation includes quickstart guides and examples for the pythonic API.\n\n## Installation\n\nThe Python bindings for cvc5 are available via PyPI, with the latest version being 1.3.3 released February 25, 2026.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"08a05c30-fdde-490a-97a9-405cd2ce18d9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:34.974Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"604dac6c-43d4-4de7-9247-0df0046dfade","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"120a0c80-dd46-4bf6-a61f-d6dc0587a2c1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a15189ea6dd761cfe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gb976ZCtaf36bx2pFhAHnf","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Bitwuzla SMT solver bit-vector 2025 benchmark performance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Available CAV Evaluation Artifact Reusable CAV Evaluation Artifact Bitwuzla ⋆\",\"url\":\"https://cs.stanford.edu/~niemetz/publications/2023/NiemetzP-CAV23.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Scalable Bit-Blasting with Abstractions\",\"url\":\"https://bitwuzla.github.io/data/NiemetzPZ-CAV24.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Bitwuzla | An SMT solver for bit-vectors, floating-points, arrays and uninterpreted functions.\",\"url\":\"https://bitwuzla.github.io/\"},{\"title\":\"Bitwuzla system architecture. | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Bitwuzla-system-architecture_fig1_372433563\"},{\"title\":\"Bitwuzla | Computer Aided Verification\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/978-3-031-37703-7_1\"},{\"title\":\"Bitwuzla at the SMT-COMP 2022 Aina Niemetz Stanford University Mathias Preiner\",\"url\":\"https://bitwuzla.github.io/data/smtcomp2022/paper.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Bitwuzla | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-37703-7_1\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - bitwuzla/bitwuzla: Bitwuzla is a Satisfiability Modulo Theories (SMT) solver for the theories of fixed-size bit-vectors, floating-point arithmetic, arrays and uninterpreted functions and their combinations. Its name is derived from an Austrian dialect expression that can be translated as “someone who tinkers with bits”.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/bitwuzla/bitwuzla\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Bitwuzla\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/372433563_Bitwuzla\"},{\"title\":\"Scalable Bit-Blasting with Abstractions | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-65627-9_9\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Bitwuzla SMT solver's bit-vector benchmark performance:\n\n## Overview\n\nBitwuzla is a Satisfiability Modulo Theories (SMT) solver for the theories of fixed-size bit-vectors, floating-point arithmetic, arrays and uninterpreted functions and their combinations.\n\n## Performance Achievement\n\nBitwuzla won 26 out of 56 (participated) division awards at SMT-COMP 2023, and it also had strong performance in prior years, with Bitwuzla winning 32 out of 48 (participated) division awards at SMT-COMP 2022.\n\n## Recent Advances\n\nRecent research has extended Bitwuzla with abstraction-refinement techniques for improved performance. The state-of-the-art SMT solver Bitwuzla was extended with an abstraction-refinement approach that significantly improves solver performance on a variety of benchmark sets, including industrial benchmarks that arise from smart contract verification.\n\nOn specific benchmarks with this enhancement: Abstr-t (the enhanced configuration) is more than 40% faster and requires 60% less memory compared to the base Bitwuzla version on certain test sets, and on the commonly 3,138 solved benchmarks, Abstr-t is the fastest solver, closely followed by cvc5-ib, with both outperforming the other bit-blasting solvers.\n\nThe search results show information primarily from 2023-2024 academic publications rather than 2025-specific benchmarks.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"533ab357-ce05-46f6-8fe2-29ca47676c39","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:35.095Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"120a0c80-dd46-4bf6-a61f-d6dc0587a2c1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"7876bffc-0100-4f2b-bb9b-93faba845a88","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"a15189ea6dd761cfe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018gm3WtW24qNQd68ihqL5yd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Z3 unsat core proof certificate Lean LFSC verification\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"On proof generation and proof checking · Z3Prover/z3 · Discussion #4881\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/discussions/4881\"},{\"title\":\"Proofs in Satisﬁability Modulo Theories\",\"url\":\"https://leodemoura.github.io/files/SMTProofs.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Question: Proof Checking in Z3 - state of the art · Issue #4653 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/4653\"},{\"title\":\"Proof Certificates\",\"url\":\"https://kind.cs.uiowa.edu/kind2_user_docs/v2.1.1/9_other/5_proofs.html\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding how F* uses Z3 — Proof-Oriented Programming in F* documentation\",\"url\":\"https://fstar-lang.org/tutorial/book/under_the_hood/uth_smt.html\"},{\"title\":\"Proof Certificates - Kind 2 - The University of Iowa\",\"url\":\"https://kind.cs.uiowa.edu/kind2_user_doc/9_other/5_proofs.html\"},{\"title\":\"z3py unsat : proof() gives invalid usage and unsat core is empty · Issue #1137 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/1137\"},{\"title\":\"Software Verification and Analysis Using Z3\",\"url\":\"https://www.nccgroup.com/research/software-verification-and-analysis-using-z3/\"},{\"title\":\"Z3.Solver\",\"url\":\"https://z3prover.github.io/api/html/ml/Z3.Solver.html\"},{\"title\":\"Question: Proof Checking in Z3 - state of the art · Z3Prover/z3 · Discussion #5000\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/discussions/5000\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about proof certificates, unsat cores, and verification systems involving Z3, LFSC, and Lean. Here's what the search revealed:\n\n## Z3 Proof Generation and Unsat Cores\n\nMany applications require a verifier for SMT refutations, where the solver accompanies its unsat answers with a certificate that can be checked independently with much simpler and more trustworthy tools (a proof checker).\n\nThe main development in Z3 regarding proofs is to support an SMT extension of DRUP (DRAT). The format is currently producing theory lemmas for arithmetic, cardinality and PB constraints, bit-vectors, arrays, and EUF.\n\n## LFSC Proof Format and Verification\n\nCVC3 was instrumented to produce LFSC-proofs (instead of proofs in its ad hoc native format) both as a platform for experimenting with proof systems and as a way to produce certified interpolants.\n\nA proof system is formally defined in LFSC through signatures, which contain a definition of the system's language together with axioms and proof rules. The proof system used by cvc5 is defined over a number of signatures, which are included in its source code distribution.\n\n## Lean and Proof Verification\n\nLean is not a standard SMT solver; it can be used as an automatic prover like SMT solvers, but it can also be used as a proof assistant. The Lean kernel is based on dependent type theory.\n\n## DRAT Checkers in Theorem Provers\n\nFor DRAT there have been checkers developed in C, ACL2, Isabelle and Coq. With the runtime advances and already established solving in Lean, it would be a very appealing platform for the extension of DRAT.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"74dc3b90-bb49-49d2-b16d-f6a668041bbb","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:37.080Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7876bffc-0100-4f2b-bb9b-93faba845a88","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
