{"parentUuid":"80b639cd-1770-4920-a89c-e9077cecf0f1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a15189ea6dd761cfe","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01QebkxJengzbT88o4fGUquS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RSKD6oi9thcH1mruK3JzW7","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"CP-SAT vs SMT no-overlap 2D placement large instances exact"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2418,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3176,"cache_read_input_tokens":31797,"output_tokens":358,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3176},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2418,"output_tokens":358,"cache_read_input_tokens":31797,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3176,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3176,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaorkaDNUMwZca6Jw3gpZ","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"43520b48-b2ac-4984-891e-1ef403986995","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:54.421Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ac4cba4f-b38f-4634-9c03-0c983424a4bc","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"58edd382-d924-48ac-8751-7dee8400aa54","agentId":"a15189ea6dd761cfe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JiDVmegYFFeMtY3vGo3izt","type":"tool_result","content":"# SMT-COMP 2025 Results Categories\n\n## Available Divisions\n\nThe competition features numerous logic divisions organized by problem type:\n\n**Arithmetic-based:** Arith, FPArith, QF_LinearIntArith, QF_LinearRealArith, QF_NonLinearIntArith, QF_NonLinearRealArith\n\n**Bitvector-based:** Bitvec, QF_Bitvec\n\n**Equality and combinations:** Equality, QF_Equality, QF_Equality+Bitvec, QF_Equality+LinearArith, QF_Equality+NonLinearArith\n\n**Mixed theories:** Equality+LinearArith, Equality+MachineArith, Equality+NonLinearArith, QF_Equality+Bitvec+Arith, QF_ADT+BitVec, QF_ADT+LinArith\n\n**Other:** QF_Datatypes, QF_FPArith, QF_Strings\n\n## Tracks\n\nEach division competes across multiple tracks: Single Query, Incremental, Unsat Core, Model Validation, and Parallel.\n\n## QF_LIA and QF_NIA Results\n\nThe page lists **QF_LinearIntArith** (QF_LIA) and **QF_NonLinearIntArith** (QF_NIA) with results available across Incremental, Model Validation, Parallel, Single Query, and Unsat Core tracks.\n\n**Note:** No winner names are displayed on this overview page; specific results require accessing individual track links."}]},"uuid":"2a60b2c7-b0f6-4620-86f1-a3a6e40271d2","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:56.361Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ac4cba4f-b38f-4634-9c03-0c983424a4bc","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"8b117678-2165-466a-b019-5bc597ecbfd4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"58edd382-d924-48ac-8751-7dee8400aa54","agentId":"a15189ea6dd761cfe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WM55EWkWQLPx5QHC1cykJT","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"OptiMathSAT\" optimization SMT solver pseudo-boolean placement\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"OptiMathSAT: A Tool for Optimization Modulo Theories ⋆\",\"url\":\"https://disi.unitn.it/rseba/papers/cav15_extended.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"OptiMathSAT: A Tool for Optimization Modulo Theories | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-21690-4_27\"},{\"title\":\"The OptiMathSAT OMT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://optimathsat.disi.unitn.it/pages/releasenotes.html\"},{\"title\":\"νZ - An Optimizing SMT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/nbjorner-nuz.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"OptiMathSAT: A Tool for Optimization Modulo Theories | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/329691575_OptiMathSAT_A_Tool_for_Optimization_Modulo_Theories\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] OptiMathSAT: A Tool for Optimization Modulo Theories | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/OptiMathSAT:-A-Tool-for-Optimization-Modulo-Sebastiani-Trentin/94b2dc584e2a720d8049ce4261b350fce0e670d2\"},{\"title\":\"Symbolic Optimization with SMT Solvers Yi Li University of Toronto\",\"url\":\"https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~aws/papers/popl14.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Optimization Modulo the Theory of Floating-Point Numbers | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-29436-6_33\"},{\"title\":\"On Optimization Modulo Theories, MaxSMT and Sorting Networks | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/313481073_On_Optimization_Modulo_Theories_MaxSMT_and_Sorting_Networks\"},{\"title\":\"νZ - An Optimizing SMT Solver | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/301991235_nZ_-_An_Optimizing_SMT_Solver\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found about OptiMathSAT:\n\n## Overview\n\nOptiMathSAT is an OMT tool extending the MathSAT5 SMT solver. OptiMathSAT allows for solving a list of optimization problems on SMT formulas with linear objective functions –on the Boolean, the rational and the integer domains, and on their combination thereof– including MaxSMT.\n\n## Pseudo-Boolean and Optimization Capabilities\n\nThe solver supports SMT with Pseudo-Boolean Objectives (PB-SMT), which is one of its key features. The solver includes automatic advanced MaxSMT/Pseudo-Boolean encoding with options for different encoding strategies, including sorting network circuit encoding with complexity O(n*log(n)*log(n)).\n\n## Multiple Objectives\n\nMultiple objective functions can be combined together and handled either independently, or lexicographically, or in a min-max/max-min fashion.\n\n## Regarding Placement\n\nWhile the search results do not directly address \"placement\" in the context of OptiMathSAT, the solver's pseudo-boolean and optimization capabilities have been applied to various problem domains including design and resource allocation tasks, which often involve placement problems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"b3e93b14-47d6-4d0a-8987-102baad69e95","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:42:59.140Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8b117678-2165-466a-b019-5bc597ecbfd4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"43520b48-b2ac-4984-891e-1ef403986995","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"58edd382-d924-48ac-8751-7dee8400aa54","agentId":"a15189ea6dd761cfe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RSKD6oi9thcH1mruK3JzW7","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT vs SMT no-overlap 2D placement large instances exact\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT: Parameter documentation NoOverlap2D, Cumulative · google/or-tools · Discussion #3107\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/discussions/3107\"},{\"title\":\"Placement of products on carrier, layer by layer with CP\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/d/topic/or-tools-discuss/T9DUhs2WCao\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Yet Another Math Programming Consultant: 2d bin packing with Google OR-Tools CP-SAT, how to fix?\",\"url\":\"http://yetanothermathprogrammingconsultant.blogspot.com/2021/02/2d-bin-packing-with-google-or-tools-cp.html\"},{\"title\":\"Differential cryptanalysis with SAT, SMT, MILP, and CP\",\"url\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/105.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT for scheduling Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://schedulingseminar.com/presentations/SchedulingSeminar_LaurentPerron.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04B_advanced_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"2D Bin Packing\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/gMgPBryj1wA\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about CP-SAT vs SMT for 2D placement problems with no-overlap constraints on large instances:\n\n## CP-SAT for 2D No-Overlap Placement\n\nCP-SAT offers the `add_no_overlap_2d` constraint which is used to prevent overlaps between intervals on two dimensions, for example for packing rectangles. CP-SAT's propagation is exact, and the CP-SAT solver with the no_overlap_2d constraint is recommended for 2D bin packing problems.\n\nKey technical characteristics of CP-SAT:\n- The propagation is exact, even though the LP solver is inexact, as it's only used as a \"hint\".\n- CP-SAT uses only int64 arithmetic with no epsilon, though it must deal with integer overflow.\n\n## SMT vs Other Approaches\n\nThe search results contain limited specific comparisons between CP-SAT and SMT for 2D placement problems. However, one comparative study found that SAT solvers were the overall winners in comparison to SMT and other approaches, with Kissat dominating the SAT category and Yices2 the SMT pool. That said, CP obtained a victory when enumerating trails of a differential for Midori.\n\nThe search results don't contain detailed benchmarks specifically comparing CP-SAT with SMT solvers on large-instance 2D placement problems with no-overlap constraints, so a more specialized search or academic literature might be needed for that specific comparison.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"5588d2b3-7cfb-4f58-a07b-4c694777bbdb","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:01.666Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"43520b48-b2ac-4984-891e-1ef403986995","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"80b639cd-1770-4920-a89c-e9077cecf0f1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"58edd382-d924-48ac-8751-7dee8400aa54","agentId":"a15189ea6dd761cfe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NUpVmS6d3Ruqk746Fi9rzc","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Z3 user propagator C++ Python API example custom theory propagate\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"User Propagators | Online Z3 Guide\",\"url\":\"https://microsoft.github.io/z3guide/programming/Example%20Programs/User%20Propagator/\"},{\"title\":\"User propagator - C++ API · Issue #5507 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/5507\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding user_propagator_base’s propagate method · Z3Prover/z3 · Discussion #5644\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/discussions/5644\"},{\"title\":\"Java API for custom User Propagators · Issue #6097 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/6097\"},{\"title\":\"[API Coherence] February 1, 2026 - Solver, Context, and Core APIs · Z3Prover/z3 · Discussion #8459\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/discussions/8459\"},{\"title\":\"What do the arguments to the user propagator callbacks represent? · Z3Prover/z3 · Discussion #5818\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/discussions/5818\"},{\"title\":\"Z3: user_propagator_base Class Reference\",\"url\":\"https://z3prover.github.io/api/html/classz3_1_1user__propagator__base.html\"},{\"title\":\"Navigating the Universe of Z3 Theory Solvers\",\"url\":\"http://theory.stanford.edu/~nikolaj/z3navigate.html\"},{\"title\":\"User propagator - final callback · Issue #5546 · Z3Prover/z3\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Z3Prover/z3/issues/5546\"},{\"title\":\"Satisfiability Modulo Custom Theories in Z3\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/367193795_Satisfiability_Modulo_Custom_Theories_in_Z3\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Z3 user propagators with C++ and Python API examples:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe UserPropagate API enables new decision procedures with a relatively low barrier of entry, allowing you to implement custom theories for Z3.\n\n## Key API Components\n\nThe TC solver is instantiated as a subclass of UserPropagateBase, implementing three virtual methods: push, pop, and fresh. Additionally, it adds optional callbacks for fixed, final, eq created and initializes data-structures for tracking constraints.\n\n## How It Works\n\nIf the fixed() callback is registered and if e is a Boolean or Bit-vector, it gets invoked when e is bound to a value. If the eq() callback is registered, equalities between registered expressions are reported. A consumer can use the propagate or conflict functions to invoke propagations or conflicts as a consequence of these callbacks.\n\n## Important Notes on API Availability\n\nThe Z3_solver_propagate_* family of functions enabling custom theory propagation is available in: C (complete), Java (UserPropagatorBase), Python (UserPropagatorBase), C# (UserPropagator class).\n\n## Resources\n\nFor practical examples, the documentation represents the relation outside of Z3, and you can find detailed guidance in the official Z3 Guide at the User Propagators section, along with example code in the Z3 repository demonstrating the reflexive transitive closure pattern.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"506304bc-6f46-4303-8e1c-e98f452cfdcd","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:43:01.714Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"80b639cd-1770-4920-a89c-e9077cecf0f1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
